# Sunbeam 12/16, 1912-1921.
#
# Louis Coatalen's 3,016 cc L-head four, 80 x 150 mm, 15.9 RAC hp; the 1912
# Coupe de l'Auto team finished first, second and third. About 4,950 were built
# by Sunbeam, plus about 1,781 licence-built by Rover during the First World
# War. No works overall dimensions have been found. The model was built to
# these ESTIMATES: 4.000 m long, 1.650 m wide, wheelbase 2.896 m, with the
# height taken from the body as photographed. The wheelbase is the figure to
# trust; the rest should be corrected if a works figure is ever found.
# https://www.sunbeam.org.au/?page_id=544
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunbeam_Motor_Car_Company
#
# Colours: Black, Dark Green, Maroon and Grey, as specified for this model.
#
# Weight: 13 in total (black 5, green 4, maroon 2, grey 2). Classes 2,3 and a
# low-volume prestige car, so the total stays inside the 10-15 band.
#
# Speed 80 km/h (50 mph), the National Motor Museum figure for its 1914 12/16.
# https://nationalmotormuseum.org.uk/collections/vehicles/sunbeam-1216hp/
# Comfort 60/420 (line). Classes 2,3.
# Dates: 1912-1921 with a road tail to about 1946.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/sunbeam-12-16.blend. References:
# citycars/references/sunbeam-12-16/ (gitignored). Images via Wikimedia Commons.
#
# Photographs: all four are the National Motor Museum's 1914 12/16 tourer,
# registration LM-7339.
# sunbeam1216-a.jpg front, CC BY 2.0, sv1ambo,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1914_Sunbeam_12-16hp_(5949733065).jpg
# sunbeam1216-b.jpg front three-quarter left, CC BY 2.0, sv1ambo,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1914_Sunbeam_12-16hp_(5950311940).jpg
# sunbeam1216-c.jpg front three-quarter right, CC BY 2.0, Michael Gaylard,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunbeam_12_16hp_(52713177574).jpg
# sunbeam1216-d.jpg rear three-quarter, CC BY 2.0, sv1ambo,
# https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1914_Sunbeam_12-16hp_(5950297164).jpg

